WEBVTT

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:19.720
<v Ira Glass>OK, so let's start today with this video. This woman posted it on Facebook, saying that these girls, little girls, walked up to her on the street, and after talking to them for a while, she just knew she had to record them. Zoe Chace, one of the reporters at NPR's Planet Money , saw the video online, and she's the one you showed it to me.
<v Ira Glass>好的，那我们就从这个视频开始今天的节目。一位女士把它发布在 Facebook 上，说这些小女孩在街上走向她，和她们聊了一会儿之后，她就知道自己必须把她们录下来。NPR 节目《Planet Money》的记者之一佐伊·蔡斯（Zoe Chace）在网上看到了这个视频，就是她把它展示给我的。

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:34.000
<v Zoe Chace>OK, so there's two little girls. They look to be about 10, 11-ish. They're in a parking lot, and the video opens, you don't see anybody, and then they run into the video, and they're right up in your face. And they are super cute.
<v Zoe Chace>好的，视频里有两个小女孩，看起来大概 10 岁、11 岁左右。她们在一个停车场里。视频刚开始时你还看不见人，然后她们跑进画面，直接凑到了镜头前，她们超级可爱。

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.310
<v Asia Newson>Excuse me, ma'am. Hi, how are you?
<v Asia Newson>不好意思，女士。嗨，你好吗？

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:37.330
<v Woman>I'm fine.  How are you?
<v Woman>我很好。你呢？

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:41.890
<v Asia Newson>I'm fine, too. My name is Asia Newson. The name of my company is called--
<v Asia Newson>我也很好。我叫亚希娅·纽森（Asia Newson）。我的公司名字叫——

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:47.560
<v Asia And Trainee>(TOGETHER)  Super Business Girl. Our mission is to recognize the true potential in every child--
<v Asia And Trainee>（合）超级商业女孩（Super Business Girl）。我们的使命是发掘每个孩子真正的潜力——

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:52.750
<v Asia Newson>And to develop intrinsic security that makes optimum use of their individualized talent.
<v Asia Newson>并培养内在的安全感，从而最大限度地利用他们个性化的天赋。

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:53.440
<v Trainee>Yeah.
<v Trainee>是的。

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:59.720
<v Ira Glass>Pause it for a second. So the girl who's talking right now, she's wearing big black glasses, but do they have--
<v Ira Glass>先暂停一下。说话的这个女孩戴着一副黑色大眼镜，但它们有——

00:00:59.720 --> 00:00:59.940
<v Zoe Chace>No.
<v Zoe Chace>没有。

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:00.280
<v Ira Glass>Lenses in them?
<v Ira Glass>上面有镜片吗？

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:10.740
<v Zoe Chace>Those are not real glasses. That's just part of her thing. And she has this little gap between her teeth that she puts right up into the camera and grins at it.
<v Zoe Chace>那不是真正的眼镜。那只是她个人风格的一部分。她牙齿中间有个小缝，她直接对着镜头展示并咧嘴一笑。

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:22.330
<v Asia Newson>Yeah. So let me break it down on an immensely biological basis. This awesome trainee right here is learning how to become a entrepreneur, how to make their own money and buy the things that they would need in their life.
<v Asia Newson>是的。让我从极其生物学的角度来剖析一下。这位很棒的学员正在学习如何成为一名企业家，如何赚自己的钱，以及购买生活中需要的东西。

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:26.420
<v Ira Glass>OK, so here she's gesturing at the other girl, the trainee, who's selling candles.
<v Ira Glass>好的，她现在指着另一个女孩，那个卖蜡烛的学员。

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:37.160
<v Asia Newson>Today I am selling some of my awesome candles, 'cause I'm raising money to buy my own school clothes and school supplies for the upcoming school year. Now I'm going to do a rap for you.
<v Asia Newson>今天我在卖一些我很棒的蜡烛，因为我在攒钱买我下学年的校服和学习用品。现在我要为你表演一段说唱。

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:42.320
<v Asia Newson>OK, now-- It's a lot of rhythm, see? Super Business Girl, Girl, Girl--
<v Asia Newson>好了，现在——节奏感很强，看好了？超级商业女孩，女孩，女孩——

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:49.980
<v Asia And Trainee>(TOGETHER)    Girl, Girl, Girl, Girl. Super Business Girl, Girl, Girl, Girl, Girl, Girl, Girl. Super Business Girl, Girl, Girl--
<v Asia And Trainee>（合）女孩，女孩，女孩，女孩。超级商业女孩，女孩，女孩，女孩，女孩，女孩，女孩。超级商业女孩，女孩，女孩——

00:01:49.980 --> 00:02:07.900
<v Zoe Chace>It gets in your head, actually, just the words "Super Business Girl, Girl, Girl, Girl." Like it gets in your head. It got into my head. And that is actually her company. Like, that is her branding. That is her selling her company, is just repeating the name of it again and again.
<v Zoe Chace>说真的，满脑子都是“超级商业女孩，女孩，女孩，女孩”这些词。就像它钻进了你的脑子里。它确实钻进了我的脑子。而这实际上就是她的公司。这就是她的品牌。她推销自己公司的方式就是一遍又一遍地重复它的名字。

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:14.010
<v Asia And Trainee>(TOGETHER) Girl, Girl, Girl, Girl, Girl, Girl. Super Business Girl, Girl, Girl, Girl, Girl.
<v Asia And Trainee>（合）女孩，女孩，女孩，女孩，女孩，女孩。超级商业女孩，女孩，女孩，女孩，女孩。

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:14.910
<v Asia Newson>That's it.
<v Asia Newson>就是这样。

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:15.290
<v Woman>Get it, girl.
<v Woman>干得漂亮，姑娘。

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:15.680
<v Asia Newson>Bye.
<v Asia Newson>再见。

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:16.510
<v Trainee>Bye.
<v Trainee>再见。

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:38.530
<v Zoe Chace>So at this point in the video is when she actually really introduces the thing that she's selling, which is these candles that she makes. And she just sticks the candles right into the camera. She's like, "Smell 'em." That's it.
<v Zoe Chace>视频到这里时，她才真正介绍了她卖的东西，也就是她自己做的蜡烛。她直接把蜡烛戳到镜头前，说：“闻闻看。”就是这样。

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:47.250
<v Ira Glass>So when Zoe saw this video, she watched it over and over, totally charmed by this kid. But also, as a business reporter, she had all kinds of questions.
<v Ira Glass>所以当佐伊看到这个视频时，她反复看了好几遍，完全被这个孩子迷住了。但作为一名商业记者，她也有各种各样的问题。

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:56.240
<v Zoe Chace>Like who is she? Was this real? Is this, like, a one-off? Does she do this all the time? What is Super Business Girl? Like, is that a thing?
<v Zoe Chace>比如她是睡？这是真的吗？这只是一次性的吗？她经常这样做吗？什么是“超级商业女孩”？这算是个正经事吗？

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:03.550
<v Ira Glass>So Zoe tracked her down. Her name is Asia Newson. She's 11. Super Business Girl is a thing. It's a website that sells candles.
<v Ira Glass>于是佐伊找到了她。她叫亚希娅·纽森，11 岁。“超级商业女孩”确实是个正经事，那是一个销售蜡烛的网站。

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:21.740
<v Ira Glass>Asia lives in West Detroit, but she happened to be coming to New York City to see her family that weekend, the one that Zoe called. And that also happens to be where Zoe lives. And I asked Zoe to play me the tape of their encounter, because our program today is all about business and the kinds of people who decide to go into business, and the kinds of people who succeed in business.
<v Ira Glass>亚希娅住在底特律西区，但恰好那个周末她要去纽约看望家人，佐伊就是在那时候联系她的。而那也正好是佐伊住的地方。我让佐伊播放了她们见面的录音，因为我们今天的节目正是关于商业，以及那些决定投身商业的人，和那些在商业中取得成功的人。

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:35.210
<v Zoe Chace>So we meet up on 125th Street in Harlem. And basically as soon as we meet, she's-- she's with her dad, she's with her mom, she's with her cousin. And they just book it across the street to this deli.
<v Zoe Chace>我们在哈莱姆区的 125 街见面。基本上我们一见面——她和她爸爸、妈妈、表兄弟在一起。他们直接冲过马路去了那家熟食店。

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:43.080
<v Asia Newson>We're going to get our product. I'm selling my lollipops. And I'm also selling my candles. But first I'm getting my lollipops.
<v Asia Newson>我们要去拿我们的产品。我在卖我的棒棒糖，还有蜡烛。但首先我要去拿我的棒棒糖。

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:57.650
<v Zoe Chace>Now Asia usually sells candles back home in Detroit, but candles are heavy and hard to carry. So today in New York, they're going to sell lollipops. She buys them for $0.25 per Blow Pop and sells them for $1.
<v Zoe Chace>亚希娅平时在底特律老家卖蜡烛，但蜡烛很重，不好携带。所以今天在纽约，他们打算卖棒棒糖。她以每支 25 美分的价格买入 Blow Pop 棒棒糖，然后以 1 美元卖出。

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:03.840
<v Asia Newson>It just-- it just makes sense to me. I won't have to spend so much on something, then I can sell it for more.
<v Asia Newson>这对我来说——这完全讲得通。我不必花那么多钱买东西，然后我能以更高的价格卖出去。

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:04.330
<v Woman>Mhm.
<v Woman>嗯哼。

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:06.870
<v Asia Newson>Making 300% profit.
<v Asia Newson>获得 300% 的利润。

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:24.080
<v Zoe Chace>300% profit. So that's her business model. And she is so full of confidence that she basically walks right out of the deli, turns to the group of guys that are just kind of hanging out outside on the street corner, and makes the pitch to them.
<v Zoe Chace>300% 的利润。这就是她的商业模式。她充满了自信，基本上刚走出熟食店，就转向街角的一群人，直接向他们推销。

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:36.600
<v Asia Newson>Excuse me, gentlemen. Hello, my name is Asia Newson. I'm known as Detroit's youngest entrepreneur. And the name of my company is called Super Business Girl. Today I'm selling lollipops because I'm raising money to buy my own school clothes--
<v Asia Newson>不好意思，先生们。你好，我叫亚希娅·纽森。我是底特律最年轻的企业家。我的公司叫“超级商业女孩”。今天我卖棒棒糖，因为我在筹钱买我的校服——

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:50.860
<v Ira Glass>Now of course, anybody who lives in a big city has been approached by kids-- you know, selling candy, asking for a couple bucks for it, saying it goes to their education or whatever. And Zoe's been approached, and all these guys have probably been approached. Zoe says this just feels different.
<v Ira Glass>当然，任何住在在大城市的人都被孩子搭讪过——你知道，卖糖果，要几块钱，说是为了他们的教育什么的。佐伊也被搭讪过，这些男士可能也都被搭讪过。但佐伊说这感觉完全不同。

00:04:50.860 --> 00:05:09.740
<v Zoe Chace>The way that Asia sells it is way less a sort of charity move than this kind of opportunity. Like, here I am. I am very special. You should pay attention to me. I am giving you a chance to invest in my future.
<v Zoe Chace>亚希娅的销售方式与其说是慈善，不如说是一种机会。就像是：我在这里，我非常特别，你应该关注我，我在给你一个投资我未来的机会。

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:18.590
<v Ira Glass>And yeah, you can hear on the tapes, people are charmed. As Asia works her way down the street, going into crowded barbershops and hair salons, they are happy to give to her.
<v Ira Glass>确实，你可以在录音里听到，人们被她迷住了。当亚希娅沿着街道，走进拥挤的理发店和美发沙龙时，人们都很乐意给她钱。

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:23.020
<v Asia Newson>Because you'll make a difference in my life. Thank you for listening to my presentation.
<v Asia Newson>因为你会对我的生活产生影响。谢谢你听我的演讲。

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:25.310
<v Man>That was beautiful. That was beautiful. How much?
<v Man>太棒了。这太棒了。多少钱？

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:30.120
<v Asia Newson>Well, I'm only asking for $1 investment, because you'll make a strong strong impact to my education.
<v Asia Newson>嗯，我只请求 1 美元的投资，因为你将对我的教育产生强有力的影响。

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:31.090
<v Man>I will?
<v Man>我会吗？

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:31.560
<v Asia Newson>Yeah.
<v Asia Newson>是的。

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:35.300
<v Man>[LAUGHS] You're beautiful, sugar. She's a sweetheart.
<v Man>（笑）你真漂亮，甜心。她真是个可爱的小姑娘。

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:36.880
<v Woman>Oh, well, thank you for the lollipop.
<v Woman>噢，谢谢你的棒棒糖。

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:39.330
<v Asia Newson>Thank you. I hope you have an awesome day.
<v Asia Newson>谢谢。希望你有美好的一天。

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:44.880
<v Woman>And I hope you have a blessed one as well, sweetie. I'm loving you! [LAUGHS]
<v Woman>也希望你有一个被祝福的一天，亲爱的。我爱你！[笑]

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:46.710
<v Zoe Chace>Why'd you buy it?
<v Zoe Chace>你为什么买它？

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:48.250
<v Man>Why did I buy it? To support her.
<v Man>我为什么买它？为了支持她。

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:48.530
<v Zoe Chace>Why?
<v Zoe Chace>为什么？

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:58.840
<v Man>It's a good cause. She's just very outspoken, very outgoing. Good personality.
<v Man>这是一个好的事业。她非常直率，非常外向，性格很好。

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:07.500
<v Asia Newson>The number-one thing is give energy. Two, speak very loud, be articulate, use hand gestures, and make eye contact.
<v Asia Newson>最重要的一点是给予能量。第二，说话声音要大，吐字清晰，多用手势，并进行眼神交流。

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:18.460
<v Ira Glass>Now this is what Asia tells other kids to do when she trains them. But of course, that is like somebody who's always been skinny giving advice to people on how to diet. This stuff just comes easily to her.
<v Ira Glass>这就是亚希娅训练其他孩子时教的内容。但当然，这就像一个一直很瘦的人在教别人如何节食。这些东西对她来说是与生俱来的。

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:28.300
<v Zoe Chace>It is a hard thing to teach. I saw her try to make her cousin do this pitch, who was with her, and he just couldn't handle it.
<v Zoe Chace>这很难教。我看到她试着让她表弟做这个推销，但他完全搞不定。

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:30.270
<v Ira Glass>What happened when her cousin tried?
<v Ira Glass>她表弟尝试时发生了什么？

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:36.910
<v Zoe Chace>Um, people wouldn't really respond to him, because he was kind of looking down. He wasn't really looking at people.
<v Zoe Chace>嗯，人们并没有什么回应，因为他有点低着头，没有真正看着人们。

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:37.760
<v Ira Glass>Oh, he was a kid.
<v Ira Glass>噢，毕竟是个孩子。

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:38.300
<v Zoe Chace>He-- yeah!
<v Zoe Chace>他——是的！

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:39.840
<v Zoe Chace>[BOTH LAUGH]
<v Zoe Chace>（两人笑）

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:52.530
<v Zoe Chace>He was a regular kid. He was a kid that was 10 years old and, like, embarrassed to be talking to adults.
<v Zoe Chace>他是个普通孩子。他是个 10 岁、对和成年人说话感到尴尬的孩子。

00:06:52.530 --> 00:07:03.870
<v Ira Glass>At this point, Asia has an office in a business incubator place in Detroit. She gets mentorship there, she gets classes. This, by the way, is an adult business incubator, where she is the only kid.  Everybody else is grown up.
<v Ira Glass>目前，亚希娅在底特律的一个商业孵化器里有间办公室。她在那里接受指导、上课。顺便说一下，那是一个成人商业孵化器，她是唯一的孩子。其他所有人都是成年人。

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:34.470
<v Ira Glass>Her dad is a salesman. And he's very involved in what Asia does, which Zoe says can be a little unsettling to watch, seeing an adult stage-parent their kid this way. Though she says that Asia is so clearly into what she is doing, it really does not feel bad to watch it. It's like watching a stage parent whose kid totally loves being on stage. And for Zoe, as a business reporter, what was interesting about Asia was her instinctive comprehension of a basic business principle-- a principle that some adults don't always seem to grasp.
<v Ira Glass>她爸爸是个推销员，也非常参与亚希娅所做的一切。佐伊说看到一个成年人这样操纵孩子可能会让人有点不安。尽管她说，很明显亚希娅非常喜欢她正在做的事情，这看起来并不让人反感。就像看着一个虎妈，而她的孩子完全热爱舞台一样。对于佐伊这位商业记者来说，亚希娅最有趣的地方在于她对基本商业原则的直觉理解——一个有时连成年人都无法领会的原则。

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:55.440
<v Zoe Chace>She has this kind of sophisticated understanding, it seems like, that people are just excited to invest in other people. Like it has really nothing to do with what Asia is selling, you know? Asia's selling Blow Pops that nobody wants. It's so obvious that she knows that she's just selling Asia.
<v Zoe Chace>她似乎有一种复杂的理解，人们只是乐于投资他人。这真的和亚希娅卖什么毫无关系，你知道吗？亚希娅卖的是没人想要的 Blow Pops。很明显，她知道她推销的其实就是她自己。

00:07:55.440 --> 00:08:10.320
<v Zoe Chace>And this is not something that all people that are making pitches to investors or customers or whatever get. Like, a lot of people are kind of nerds, and they're like, "This is my product. This is my platform. I built this app, or whatever it is, and I want people--"
<v Zoe Chace>而这并不是所有向投资者或客户推销的人都能意识到的。很多人有点书呆子气，他们会说：“这是我的产品，这是我的平台。我构建了这个应用，等等，我想要人们——”

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:13.360
<v Ira Glass>Right. They think the thing will speak on its merits. They have a great product.
<v Ira Glass>对，他们认为产品本身会说话。他们有很棒的产品。

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:23.170
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah, or that the idea is so special. But they don't realize that people connect to other people. But Asia knows this.
<v Zoe Chace>是的，或者认为这个想法非常特别。但他们没有意识到人们是与他人建立联系的。但亚希娅知道这一点。

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:27.060
<v Zoe Chace>Do you think that you're selling more, like, you're selling, you know--
<v Zoe Chace>你觉得你是在卖——你是说，你卖的是——

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:34.220
<v Asia Newson>More of myself and my services than my product? Yeah, I do. Or was that not what you were going to say? People ask me that question all the time.
<v Asia Newson>卖的是我自己和我的服务，多过我的产品？是的，我是这么认为的。或者那不是你想问的？人们一直问我这个问题。

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:35.620
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah, that's what I was gonna ask.
<v Zoe Chace>是的，我本来就是想问这个。

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:37.020
<v Asia Newson>That's what I thought.
<v Asia Newson>我就是这么想的。

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:47.300
<v Zoe Chace>I wasn't even there yet with my question. I was trying to tease this thing out of her the way you do, especially if it's a little kid. But she already knows. She already knows what she is.
<v Zoe Chace>我还没问到那儿呢。我本来想像对待小孩子那样慢慢引导她说出来，但她已经知道了。她已经知道她自己是什么了。

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:51.460
<v Asia Newson>I was a cute girl who knew about business and who liked what I did.
<v Asia Newson>我是一个懂得商业且喜欢自己所做之事的可爱女孩。

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:52.990
<v Zoe Chace>So you know that you're cute.
<v Zoe Chace>所以你知道你很可爱。

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:53.800
<v Asia Newson>Yeah.
<v Asia Newson>是的。

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:56.530
<v Zoe Chace>And how do you use the being cute in your business?
<v Zoe Chace>你是如何利用可爱在你的生意中的？

00:08:56.530 --> 00:09:16.240
<v Asia Newson>[GIGGLES] Um-- [GIGGLES] I just act like me. I'm a little girl running my own business. That's the best advantage ever. But I'm 11 now, so as I'm getting older, it's kind of going away a little bit, which makes it a little harder for me to sell my candles and my suckers.
<v Asia Newson>（咯咯笑）嗯——（咯咯笑）我只是做我自己。我是一个经营自己生意的女孩。那是最好的优势。但我现在 11 岁了，随着我长大，这种优势正在逐渐消失，这让推销蜡烛和棒棒糖变得有点困难了。

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:19.450
<v Ira Glass>Oh, no! She's getting older and she's losing it at 11?
<v Ira Glass>噢，不！她 11 岁就开始觉得自己不再可爱了吗？

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:22.570
<v Zoe Chace>(LAUGHING)  Yeah. She knows that she's not gonna be cute forever.
<v Zoe Chace>（笑）是的。她知道她不可能永远可爱下去。

00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:26.050
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait, wait, did she make more money when she was littler?
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等，她小时候赚的钱更多吗？

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:36.680
<v Zoe Chace>(LAUGHING)  Yeah. She remembers being five and making a sales pitch, and people were more excited to give her money the littler she was.
<v Zoe Chace>（笑）是的。她记得五岁时做推销，她越小，人们给钱就越兴奋。

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:59.460
<v Ira Glass>Luckily as she ages, she also has charisma, mentors, and plans-- big, big plans. And today on our radio show, "It's Not the Product, It is the Person," we have stories of adults who do not always seem to understand that idea as well as this 11-year-old girl does. And also of adults who do understand that they're the product, and they are struggling to live up to the job of being the product.
<v Ira Glass>幸运的是，随着年龄增长，她还有个人魅力、导师和计划——非常宏大的计划。今天在我们的广播节目《不是产品，是人》中，我们将分享一些成年人的故事，他们似乎并不像这个 11 岁的女孩那样理解这一理念。还有些人理解自己就是产品，但他们却在努力维持这种身为“产品”的工作。

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:21.690
<v Ira Glass>Just think for a second-- how does it make you feel when you know you are the product and the world is deciding not to buy? From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>试想一下——当你明知自己是产品而世界决定不买账时，那是什么感觉？来自芝加哥 WBEZ 电台，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。请继续收听。

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:46.300
<v Ira Glass>Act One, "I Got 99 Problems and a Pitch is One." So here's a thought that's got a lot of people into a lot of trouble-- the thought, "I can do that." I can land that skateboard trick. I can start my own band. I think I want to open a bookstore. You know, I've always loved books. I could own a bookstore. There's just that moment when you go from observer to doer, can be very, very treacherous.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“我有 99 个问题，推销是其中之一”。人们经常陷入一个麻烦的想法：“我能做到”。我能完成那个滑板技巧。我能组建自己的乐队。我想开一家书店。你知道，我一直喜欢书，我能拥有书店。当你从旁观者变成实干家时，那一刻可能会非常、非常危险。

00:10:46.300 --> 00:11:00.010
<v Ira Glass>Well, This American Life producer Alex Blumberg is in the middle of this moment right now, an extreme version of this moment. For several years now, Alex has been running Planet Money . That's the podcast about economics and business that Zoe Chace works for, who you heard just a minute ago.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》的制作人亚历克斯·布隆伯格（Alex Blumberg）现在正处于这一时刻，这是一个极端版本。几年来，亚历克斯一直在运营《Planet Money》。也就是佐伊·蔡斯所在的那个关于经济和商业的播客。

00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:26.420
<v Ira Glass>Recently, Alex quit that gig at Planet Money . He stopped reporting on other people's businesses, and he decided to start his own business instead. And he started recording the process of making a new business with his audio gear, because although he really doesn't know much about running a business, he knows a lot about recording conversations, and he felt like he needed something to feel good about. He has this true account of one man's journey from man to businessman.
<v Ira Glass>最近，亚历克斯辞去了《Planet Money》的工作。他不再报道别人的生意，而是决定自己创业。他用音频设备记录下了创业的全过程，因为虽然他对经营生意了解不多，但他非常擅长记录谈话，而且他觉得需要一些让自己感觉良好的事。这是一个人从平凡人走向企业家的真实记录。

00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:39.770
<v Alex Blumberg>One of the great things about reporting on yourself? All the access you get. As a business reporter reporting on myself as a businessman, I would be privy to all this behind-the-scenes stuff that most businesspeople never, ever talk to reporters about.
<v Alex Blumberg>报道自己的一大好处是什么？你能获得所有的权限。作为一名报道自己的商业记者，我能接触到大多数商业人士绝不会告诉记者的幕后事。

00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:57.270
<v Alex Blumberg>For example, this detail. When you're going out to do something you've never done before-- meet an investor at a venture capital firm and try to convince him to give you money to start your company-- what do you wear? Suit and tie? Tech golf-shirt casual? I was on my way out the door, and my wife Nazanin stopped me and told me to change my shoes.
<v Alex Blumberg>比如这个细节：当你准备去做一件从未做过的事情——去风险投资公司见投资者，并试图说服他给你钱创业时——你穿什么？西装革履？科技公司的休闲高尔夫衫？我正要出门时，我妻子纳扎宁（Nazanin）拦住我，让我换鞋。

00:11:57.270 --> 00:12:05.440
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>They're fine. They're just-- there'd be a higher chance that he's going to give you money if you're not wearing running shoes. Don't you think that's true?
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>它们挺好的。只是——如果你不穿跑鞋的话，他给你钱的机会会更大。你不觉得吗？

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:11.120
<v Alex Blumberg>But these are the only shoes that I can wear that my feet don't hurt in. [LAUGHS]
<v Alex Blumberg>但这是我穿起来脚不痛的唯一一双鞋了。（笑）

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:12.540
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>Um.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>嗯。

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:30.610
<v Alex Blumberg>Like a lot of conversations between married people, what we're talking about is not actually what we're talking about. Here's what we're actually talking about. We have a two-year-old and a four-year-old. My wife has a demanding job where she works till 11 o'clock most nights a week. I'm leaving a full-time job with a good salary and benefits for an uncertain future.
<v Alex Blumberg>就像很多已婚夫妇的谈话一样，我们谈论的实际上并不是我们正在谈论的内容。我们实际在讨论的是：我们有一个两岁和一个四岁的孩子。我妻子有一份高要求的工作，每周大部分晚上要工作到 11 点。我正要放弃一份薪水丰厚、福利完善的全职工作，去迎接一个不确定的未来。

00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:43.280
<v Alex Blumberg>Our plan, if you can call it that, is to spend down our meager savings, go into debt, and hope it works out. I have a lot of anxiety. Nazanin has a lot of anxiety-- which is all focused, at this moment, on which shoes I should wear.
<v Alex Blumberg>我们的计划——如果这算计划的话——就是花光我们微薄的积蓄，负债，然后希望一切顺利。我有很多焦虑。纳扎宁也有很多焦虑——而在这一刻，所有的焦虑都集中在：我该穿哪双鞋。

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:51.770
<v Alex Blumberg>I don't know. You think these look better? Not sure they look better. I think these look like nursing shoes.
<v Alex Blumberg>我不知道。你觉得这双好看吗？不确定它们是否更好。我觉得它们看起来像护士穿的鞋。

00:12:51.770 --> 00:12:53.110
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>Why did you buy them?
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>你为什么买它们？

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:54.820
<v Alex Blumberg>To play tennis in. [LAUGHS]
<v Alex Blumberg>为了打网球。（笑）

00:12:54.820 --> 00:12:56.600
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>Oh, they're like actual tennis shoes.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>噢，它们是真正的网球鞋啊。

00:12:56.600 --> 00:12:57.740
<v Alex Blumberg>Yes.
<v Alex Blumberg>是的。

00:12:57.740 --> 00:13:00.050
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>Oh, OK. Then, yeah, wear the other ones.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>噢，好的。那好吧，穿另外一双吧。

00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:06.660
<v Alex Blumberg>[LAUGHS]
<v Alex Blumberg>（笑）

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:27.480
<v Ira Glass>There are over 28 million businesses in America. And I imagine they almost all started the way mine is-- with nothing but a story, a story I'm telling not just to my investors, but to my wife and to myself. That story that I'm telling, that we all probably tell, I'm the guy in the garage with the great idea. I am Steve Jobs.
<v Ira Glass>美国有超过 2800 万家企业。我想它们几乎都是这样开始的——只有故事，一个我不仅告诉投资者，也告诉妻子和自己的故事。那个故事我们可能都讲过：我是车库里有绝妙想法的家伙。我是史蒂夫·乔布斯。

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:39.570
<v Ira Glass>Just not the actual Steve Jobs, of course. I am the Steve Jobs of HVAC repair, or the Steve Jobs of farm-to-table gastropubs. Or, in my case, the Steve Jobs of 20- to 40-minute weekly podcasts.
<v Ira Glass>当然，不是真正的史蒂夫·乔布斯。我是 HVAC（暖通空调）维修界的乔布斯，或是从农场到餐桌的餐厅界的乔布斯。或者，以我为例，我是 20 到 40 分钟每周播客界的乔布斯。

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:56.300
<v Ira Glass>That is my business. I love podcasts. I want more of them. I looked around and there didn't seem to be anybody, inside or outside of public radio, devoted to making the kind of podcasts that I love. And then came the other thought. "Well, I could do that."
<v Ira Glass>这就是我的生意。我热爱播客。我想要更多。我环顾四周，似乎无论是公共广播内部还是外部，都没有人致力于制作我喜欢的这类播客。然后那个念头又来了：“好吧，我能做到。”

00:13:56.300 --> 00:14:09.300
<v Ira Glass>The story, though, that we tell ourselves is a lie. Most of us are, in fact, not even close to being Steve Jobs. Of the hundreds of thousands of businesses that start every year, only 3 in 10 survive out the decade.
<v Ira Glass>然而，我们告诉自己的故事是个谎言。事实上，我们大多数人甚至连成为史蒂夫·乔布斯的边都沾不上。每年创办的数十万家企业中，只有 30% 能存活超过十年。

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:10.260
<v Alex Blumberg>Chris?
<v Alex Blumberg>克里斯？

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:10.870
<v Chris Sacca>How are you?
<v Chris Sacca>你好吗？

00:14:10.870 --> 00:14:11.680
<v Alex Blumberg>Hey, how's it going?
<v Alex Blumberg>嘿，怎么样？

00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:12.630
<v Chris Sacca>Good, good, good.
<v Chris Sacca>很好，很好。

00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:13.970
<v Alex Blumberg>Thanks for meeting me.
<v Alex Blumberg>谢谢你跟我见面。

00:14:13.970 --> 00:14:29.930
<v Ira Glass>It's Friday, lunch, mid-spring, and I'm at a hole-in-the-wall sushi place on Pico Boulevard in Los Angeles. I've flown out here from my home in New York to meet a guy named Chris Sacca. Chris is the kind of guy that people like me need to sell our story to. He's a professional investor.
<v Ira Glass>周五，午餐时间，春季中期，我在洛杉矶 Pico 大道一家不起眼的寿司店。我从纽约家里飞过来见一个叫克里斯·萨卡（Chris Sacca）的人。克里斯就是像我这样的人需要推销故事的对象。他是一位专业投资者。

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:46.880
<v Ira Glass>Now before I was in public radio, I was a teacher, and before that, a social worker. I've worked in nonprofits my entire life. If I actually start this business, CEO of my own company will be the first full-time job in the private sector I've ever had. All this to say, Chris Sacca and I traditionally run in different circles.
<v Ira Glass>在加入公共广播之前，我是一名老师，再之前是社会工作者。我一生都在非营利组织工作。如果我真的创办了这家公司，担任自己公司的首席执行官将是我第一份私营部门的全职工作。简而言之，克里斯·萨卡和我传统上属于不同的圈子。

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:59.440
<v Ira Glass>But this is where covering business helps. I met Chris on a Planet Money story I worked on about the patent system and how it seemed to be slowing innovation in Silicon Valley. Chris had a lot to say on the subject, so I ended up talking to him for a while.
<v Ira Glass>但这就是商业报道有所帮助的地方。我在《Planet Money》一篇关于专利制度的文章中遇到了克里斯，那个制度似乎正在减缓硅谷的创新。克里斯对这个话题有很多看法，所以我和他聊了很久。

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:15.030
<v Ira Glass>Years later, when I was starting this company, I reached out to him about being an investor. Honestly, he was one of the only people I knew who did this for a living, invested in stuff. And he remembered me. Turns out he's a fan of This American Life . And so he said, "Sure. Come on out. We'll talk."
<v Ira Glass>多年后，当我创办这家公司时，我联系了他，询问他是否愿意投资。老实说，他是我认识的极少数以此为生、投资项目的人之一。他记得我。原来他是《美国生活》的粉丝。所以他说：“当然。过来吧，我们聊聊。”

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:33.810
<v Ira Glass>Now I knew that Chris was successful, but I didn't realize exactly how successful until this lunch. One of Chris's first investments as an independent investor was in a company a former colleague of his at Google was starting. The colleague's name? Evan Williams. The company? Twitter. Evan showed Chris an early prototype of the idea.
<v Ira Glass>我知道克里斯很成功，但直到这顿午餐我才意识到他到底有多成功。克里斯作为独立投资者的第一笔投资，是投给他在谷歌的前同事创办的一家公司。同事的名字叫埃文·威廉姆斯（Evan Williams）。公司名叫 Twitter。埃文向克里斯展示了这个想法的早期原型。

00:15:33.810 --> 00:15:53.810
<v Chris Sacca>It was compelling, and a little bit addictive, and released dopamine every time your phone buzzed with a new tweet. And it played on a little bit of narcissism and exhibitionism and exploration-- all these things were happening that were pretty exciting. And so when Ev gave me the chance to invest in that company, I found it irresistible.
<v Chris Sacca>它引人入胜，还有点让人上瘾，每次你的手机因为新推文响起时，它就会释放多巴胺。它利用了人们的一点自恋、表演欲和探索欲——所有这些事情同时发生，非常令人兴奋。所以当 Ev 给我机会投资那家公司时，我觉得无法抗拒。

00:15:53.810 --> 00:16:15.600
<v Alex Blumberg>He wrote a check for $25,000, which he probably had no business doing. He had a little money, sure, but $25,000 was still a lot of cash to him. Unlike the majority of investors in Silicon Valley, who Chris says can break off a check for $25,000 like it's nothing, he really needed his bet to pay off. So he just started showing up at the headquarters of Twitter, trying to help out however he could.
<v Alex Blumberg>他写了一张 2.5 万美元的支票，他当时可能根本不该这么做。他确实有一点钱，但对当时的克里斯来说，2.5 万美元仍然是一大笔钱。与硅谷大多数投资者不同——他们开出 2.5 万美元支票就像没事人一样——克里斯真的需要这笔赌注获得回报。所以他开始频繁出现在 Twitter 总部，尽其所能提供帮助。

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:44.090
<v Alex Blumberg>To say that this gamble paid off is an understatement. Chris Sacca wouldn't say exactly how much he personally owns of Twitter, but at the time of Twitter's IPO, his venture fund was the single largest shareholder in the company, with a stake of roughly 15%. For Chris, what started as a single $25,000 investment is now a stake worth hundreds of millions of dollars.
<v Alex Blumberg>说这次赌博获得了回报简直是轻描淡写。克里斯·萨卡没透露他个人具体拥有多少 Twitter 的股份，但在 Twitter IPO 时，他的风险基金是该公司最大的单一股东，持股比例约为 15%。对克里斯来说，起初那笔 2.5 万美元的投资，现在变成了价值数亿美元的股份。

00:16:44.090 --> 00:17:07.190
<v Alex Blumberg>Now one Twitter in your portfolio is something any investor would dream of. Chris has a lot more than one. Kickstarter, Instagram, Uber-- he was an early investor in all of them. And he's developed something of a philosophy for successful investing. Sure, you evaluate the idea, you look at the numbers, kick the tires-- that's all important. But, he says, he also looks hard at the conviction of the people who are pitching him.
<v Alex Blumberg>投资组合中有一个 Twitter 是任何投资者梦寐以求的。克里斯拥有的不仅仅这一个。Kickstarter、Instagram、Uber——他是所有这些公司的早期投资者。他形成了一种成功的投资哲学。当然，你评估想法，看数据，进行尽职调查——这都很重要。但他表示，他也非常看重向他推销的人的信念。

00:17:07.190 --> 00:17:40.280
<v Chris Sacca>The ideas that we back and the entrepreneurs we back, there's so much conviction about the inevitability of success, it's contagious. I mean, when I first sat down with Kevin Systrom, the founder of Instagram, and he started talking about why Instagram, and pitching me on why I should get involved there, I sat down with, in the back of my mind, look, photo-sharing has been done a bunch of times. I feel lucky to have already gotten some money back out of Photobucket before. Like, I might be the one guy who's made money in photo-sharing, and there's no way lightning's going to strike twice.
<v Chris Sacca>我们支持的想法和创业者，他们对成功的必然性有着如此强烈的信念，这具有传染性。我的意思是，当我第一次与 Instagram 的创始人凯文·斯特罗姆（Kevin Systrom）坐下来时，他开始谈论为什么要做 Instagram，推销我为什么应该加入。我当时心想：嘿，照片分享已经被做过很多次了。我觉得自己能从 Photobucket 那里拿回一些钱已经很幸运了。我可能是唯一一个在照片分享上赚到钱的人，绝不可能有第二次好运。

00:17:40.280 --> 00:18:04.280
<v Chris Sacca>But as you listen to him, you get the perception that he's actually looking through you to some spot behind you that's five years in the future. And he just knows the inevitability of the success of his platform. And by the end of the conversation, you're like, please take my money. So what starts as this, like, all right, kid, what-- whaddaya got? It's just, like, wow, let me get on this thing. The train is leaving the station.
<v Chris Sacca>但当你听他说话时，你会有一种感觉，他仿佛透过你看向五年后的未来。他知道他的平台注定会成功。到谈话结束时，你会想：请拿走我的钱吧。所以起初那种“好吧，孩子，你有什么？”的态度，变成了“哇，让我加入吧，列车要出发了。”

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:15.020
<v Alex Blumberg>This was thrilling to hear, because that conviction, I actually feel it. And conviction, in general, is a pretty rare feeling for me. I'm much more intimate with doubt and ambivalence.
<v Alex Blumberg>听到这些令人兴奋，因为那种信念，我确实感受到了。而且，总的来说，信念对我来说是一种相当罕见的感觉。我更熟悉的是怀疑和矛盾。

00:18:15.020 --> 00:18:33.490
<v Alex Blumberg>And Chris is like a teacher handing me the answers to a test he's about to give, explaining exactly what he wants to see from me in order to invest in my company. I need to project conviction. Check. And I need to instill FOMO. For you non-Millennials, FOMO is an acronym-- Fear Of Missing Out.
<v Alex Blumberg>克里斯就像老师把即将到来的考试答案递给我，明确解释他想从我身上看到什么才能投资我的公司。我需要展现信念。打钩。我还需要灌输 FOMO。对于非千禧一代，FOMO 是一个缩写——错失恐惧症（Fear Of Missing Out）。

00:18:33.490 --> 00:18:55.000
<v Chris Sacca>Airbnb, multi-billion-dollar business, right? I was one of the first people to see the Airbnb page. And I pulled them aside and said, guys, this is super dangerous. You're renting out a room in somebody's house while they're still there? Somebody's going to get raped or murdered, and the blood is gonna be on your hands. There's no way this'll succeed. That's a $10 billion business today that I'm not an investor in.
<v Chris Sacca>Airbnb，数十亿美元的公司，对吧？我是最早看到 Airbnb 页面的人之一。我把他们拉到一边说，伙计们，这太危险了。你们在别人还在家的时候出租房间？一定会有人被强奸或杀害，你们手上有血。这绝不可能成功。今天那是家 100 亿美元的公司，而我不是投资者。

00:18:55.000 --> 00:19:08.970
