<v Chris Sacca>我可以从另一方面来说。是的，是的，是的。媒体非常非常难，如你所知。这种内容很难消费。这是长篇幅的。你指望人们花费那种专门的时间。我的意思是，你在与注意力的总体趋势作斗争，其他所有格式都在往越来越短的内容发展。所以你实际上是在逆水行舟。

00:27:45.380 --> 00:28:15.510
<v Chris Sacca>You have a lot of platform risk, because you're depending upon Apple and Google to distribute your content. The kind of stuff you're doing with Planet Money is exciting, but it's all under the veil of being a nonprofit. People feel a moral obligation to contribute to those kinds of things. You come outside from under the public radio veil, and you have to worry about whether people are going to be a little bit jaded and feel like they don't necessarily need to donate to a for-profit company to make for-profit content for guys who are doing this for equity and hoping to get rich.
<v Chris Sacca>你有很大的平台风险，因为你依赖苹果和谷歌来分发内容。你在《Planet Money》做的事情很令人兴奋，但那都是在非营利组织的遮羞布下进行的。人们觉得有道德义务为这类事情做贡献。你走出公共广播的遮羞布，你就得担心人们是否会有点愤世嫉俗，觉得他们不必一定要捐钱给一家营利性公司，去制作营利性内容，给那些寻求股权并希望发财的人。

00:28:15.510 --> 00:28:38.770
<v Chris Sacca>You know, frankly, you think you might actually be uniquely qualified to do this, but we've started to see more and more news podcasts make their way out there. Some stuff from Khan Academy is moving up the ladder right now. So ultimately, you're not going to be the only guys in here, and it's going to be pretty competitive. Audio is kind of a niche market, and so I don't know. I don't-- I don't know if this is a fit for us.
<v Chris Sacca>你知道，坦白说，你觉得你可能确实有独特的资格做这件事，但我们已经开始看到越来越多的新闻播客涌现出来。可汗学院（Khan Academy）的一些内容现在正在向上爬。所以归根结底，你不会是唯一的玩家，竞争会相当激烈。音频算是一个小众市场，所以我不知道。我不知道——我不知道这是否适合我们。

00:28:38.770 --> 00:28:54.350
<v Alex Blumberg>At this point, I have no idea what to think. I'm drained, my pits are drenched, and Chris Sacca has just given me two completely convincing cases in favor of and against investing in my business. Whatever shred of conviction I had about this process at the beginning is gone.
<v Alex Blumberg>在这一点上，我不知道该想什么。我筋疲力尽，腋下湿透，克里斯·萨卡刚刚给了我两个关于是否投资我的生意、完全令人信服的案例。我在开始时对这个过程所持有的那点信念已经荡然无存。

00:28:54.350 --> 00:29:11.330
<v Alex Blumberg>I also learned something about how investors like Chris see the companies they might invest in. I came out here thinking I could build a nice profitable business, but Chris isn't looking for profitable. He's looking for Twitter, something huge. Or if not Twitter, then at least a company he could sell to Twitter or some other big company.
<v Alex Blumberg>我也学到了一些关于克里斯这样的投资者如何看待他们可能投资的公司的东西。我来到这里以为自己能建立一家不错的营利性公司，但克里斯并不在寻找“营利”。他在寻找下一个 Twitter，那种巨大的东西。或者如果不是 Twitter，至少是一家他可以卖给 Twitter 或其他大公司从而退出的小公司。

00:29:11.330 --> 00:29:24.010
<v Alex Blumberg>He asked me this question. What would the exit be? And by that he means, what large company will buy your company in three to five years so investors like me can get our money back at 10 to 100 times the amount we put in?
<v Alex Blumberg>他问了我这个问题。退出的方式是什么？他的意思是，哪家大公司会在三到五年内买下你的公司，好让我们这些投资者能以投入金额 10 到 100 倍的回报拿回我们的钱？

00:29:24.010 --> 00:29:36.200
<v Alex Blumberg>I hadn't really thought about that question. I don't know if I want anyone to buy me. This experience with Chris makes me realize this simple vision I have, it's not a vision people like him are looking to invest in.
<v Alex Blumberg>我真的没考虑过那个问题。我不知道我是否想让任何人买下我。这次与克里斯的经历让我意识到，我拥有的这个简单愿景，并不是他这类人想要投资的愿景。

00:29:36.200 --> 00:29:56.350
<v Alex Blumberg>This was my big chance, a huge Silicon Valley investor with enough money to snap his fingers and get me started on my new life, and I'd blown it. My failure, it seemed clear, had nothing to do with my shoes.
<v Alex Blumberg>这是我的绝佳机会，一位硅谷大投资者，有足够的钱只要打个响指就能让我开始新生活，而我却把它搞砸了。我的失败，似乎很明显，与我的鞋子毫无关系。

