<v Ira Glass>The "StartUp" podcast is that hearstartup.com. That's hear, H-E-A-R, startup.com. Coming up, Mike Birbiglia and why anyone starting a business lives low to the ground. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>《StartUp》播客网址是 hearstartup.com。那是 hear，H-E-A-R，startup.com。接下来，迈克·比尔比利亚（Mike Birbiglia）以及为什么任何创业的人都要活得脚踏实地。稍后继续收听，来自芝加哥公共广播的节目。

00:31:12.690 --> 00:31:38.310
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show, "It's the Person, Not the Product." We have stories of businesses in their very early stages and the people trying to start them, and what happens when you know the most important thing you need to do is to win over people, make friends, influence people, and you also know you are not doing it. We've arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two, "The Business of Show."
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周我们的节目都会选择一个主题，为您带来关于该主题的不同类型的故事。今天的节目主题是“是人，而不是产品”。我们有关于创业初期以及那些试图开创它们的人的故事，还有当你明知你需要做的最重要的事情是赢取人心、结交朋友、影响他人，而你又明知自己没做到时会发生什么。我们已经到达了节目的第二幕。第二幕，“表演的生意”。

00:31:38.310 --> 00:31:57.710
<v Mike Birbiglia>Being a comedian, I have to say, is-- it's a little bit like starting a business where no one wants that business to exist. Really. Like, no one. Not your family, or your friends, or the audience. And in so many ways, they're right.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我必须说，成为一名喜剧演员有点像开一家没人希望它存在的公司。真的。就像，没人希望。你的家人、朋友，还是观众。而在很多方面，他们是对的。

00:31:57.710 --> 00:32:23.580
<v Ira Glass>They're right, of course, because you are bad. That is comedian Mike Birbiglia. And he says at first you just do not know what you're doing. And he says that the normal laws of supply and demand, they apply to the comedy business. And so at first, there is no demand for you and your services. He tells this story from his early days trying to start the business of his own comedy career out of nothing, with nobody believing in him.
<v Ira Glass>当然，他们是对的，因为你很烂。那位是喜剧演员迈克·比尔比利亚。他说起初你根本不知道自己在做什么。他说供需的基本法则同样适用于喜剧行业。所以起初，人们对你和你的服务没有需求。他讲述了他早期尝试从零开始建立自己的喜剧事业的故事，当时没人相信他。

00:32:23.580 --> 00:32:43.620
<v Mike Birbiglia>When I moved to New York 13 years ago to become a comedian, my parents were very upset, because my-- and they really, really were. They were very concerned. My dad is a doctor, my mom is a nurse-- those are professions where people help other people and are ashamed of their artistic children.
<v Mike Birbiglia>当我 13 年前搬到纽约成为一名喜剧演员时，我的父母非常沮丧，因为我的——他们真的、真的非常担心。我爸爸是医生，我妈妈是护士——那些职业里的人都在帮助他人，并为他们那艺术家的孩子感到羞耻。

00:32:43.620 --> 00:32:53.220
<v Mike Birbiglia>But my parents were so worried when I moved to New York. And in fairness, I mean, I was really struggling. I was broke. I was living on an air mattress in Queens. I couldn't afford a dresser for my clothes.
<v Mike Birbiglia>但我搬到纽约时，父母非常担心。说实话，我当时确实在挣扎。我破产了，住在皇后区的一张充气床垫上。我买不起放衣服的梳妆台。

00:32:53.220 --> 00:33:17.220
<v Mike Birbiglia>When you're broke, everything is low to the ground, you know? Like, you roll off your air mattress in the morning. You grab some pants from the floor. You cook noodles on a hot plate. One falls out of your mouth, you're like, "Aw, it's not too far!" You know?
<v Mike Birbiglia>当你身无分文时，一切都贴近地面，你知道吗？就像，你早上从充气床垫上滚下来，从地板上抓起裤子，在电炉上煮面条。有一根面条从嘴里掉出来，你会觉得，“噢，没掉多远！”你知道吗？

00:33:17.220 --> 00:33:47.950
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I couldn't even get past at the clubs in New York City, which is the real only reason I moved there. So I drove my mom's station wagon around the country to areas of lesser comedy concentration, places like Ohio and West Virginia and New Jersey. And I bought my mom's car. That's an important detail. Because I found out recently that sometimes when people's parents have faith in their child's dreams, they'll just give them their car.
<v Mike Birbiglia>而且我甚至无法在纽约的俱乐部演出，那是我搬到那里的唯一真正原因。所以我开着我妈妈的旅行车在全国各地跑，去那些喜剧浓度较低的地区，像俄亥俄州、西弗吉尼亚州和新泽西州。我买了我妈妈的车。这是一个重要的细节。因为我最近发现，有时候当父母对孩子的梦想有信心时，他们会直接把车送给他们。

00:33:47.950 --> 00:34:04.600
<v Mike Birbiglia>My mom upcharged me on the station wagon. She got the full Blue Book value for the vehicle. She was like, this guy just doesn't have it. I gotta get the short money out now.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我妈妈在旅行车上加了价。她拿到了那辆车的全额二手车估价。她当时想，这家伙没戏，我现在得把能赚的快钱赚到手。

00:34:04.600 --> 00:34:34.650
<v Mike Birbiglia>I remember this particularly low point for me when I was performing in a comedy club in New Jersey, and I was driving back down the ramp into the Lincoln Tunnel, and towards my air mattress in Queens. And the cop just sort of waved me over. You realize, when you're broke, that cops will just pull you over if you have a crappy car to see what else you've got going wrong in your life. They'll just be like, "You! Come here. We're going to figure out what you've done."
<v Mike Birbiglia>我记得这对我来说是一个特别低谷的时刻，当时我在新泽西的一家喜剧俱乐部表演，我沿着斜坡开回林肯隧道，朝我皇后区的充气床垫开去。警察示意我靠边停车。你意识到，当你破产时，如果你的车很烂，警察会直接拦下你，看看你生活中还有什么别的问题。他们会说，“你！过来。我们要弄清楚你到底做了什么。”

00:34:34.650 --> 00:34:51.260
<v Mike Birbiglia>And the cop says, "License and registration." As I'm pulling out my license, I'm realizing that it is expired by three weeks. So he was right.
<v Mike Birbiglia>警察说：“驾照和行车证。”当我拿出驾照时，我意识到它已经过期三周了。所以他是对的。

00:34:51.260 --> 00:35:08.280
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I had that moment where I'm thinking, wait, should I preface this conversation with this key piece of information? Like, "All right, you're not gonna like this!" You know, like, "Same team! We both think I'm an idiot!"
<v Mike Birbiglia>那一刻我在想，等等，我应该先说出这个关键信息吗？比如，“好吧，你不会喜欢这个的！”你知道，就像，“同一队！我们都觉得我是个白痴！”

00:35:08.280 --> 00:35:25.710
<v Mike Birbiglia>I didn't mention it. He goes back to the cruiser. He's there for 15 minutes, because there's a lot to do. And then when he comes back, he's a little more stern. He says, "Did you know that your license is suspended?"
<v Mike Birbiglia>我没提这事。他回到警车里。他在那里待了 15 分钟，因为有很多工作要做。当他回来时，他变得严厉了一些。他说：“你知道你的驾照被吊销了吗？”

00:35:25.710 --> 00:35:59.000
<v Mike Birbiglia>Yeah. See, I didn't-- I didn't know what suspended meant. I was very naive. Apparently I had an unpaid speeding ticket from high school, and my family moved. They don't forward registry mail, and so I said to him, and I quote, I said, "Yes. No. Wait, what does suspended mean?"
<v Mike Birbiglia>是的。看，我没——我不知道“吊销”是什么意思。我当时很天真。显然我高中时有一张未缴纳的超速罚单，而我家里搬家了。他们不转寄挂号信，所以我对他说，引用原话，我说：“是。不。等等，吊销是什么意思？”

00:35:59.000 --> 00:36:16.310
<v Mike Birbiglia>And he said, "Please step out of the car." And I said, "No, no, no, I'm serious. I don't know what suspended means," thinking we were having away a witty repartee.
<v Mike Birbiglia>他说：“请下车。”我说：“不，不，不，我是认真的，我不知道吊销是什么意思，”以为我们在进行一场诙谐的对谈。

00:36:16.310 --> 00:36:43.300
<v Mike Birbiglia>And he says, "Please step out of the car." And so I step out of the car. And he says, "You're under arrest." And I said, "I don't think so." And he says, "Yes, I'm sure of it." And then he cuffs me up against the car and put me in the back of the cruiser.
<v Mike Birbiglia>他又说：“请下车。”于是我下了车。他说：“你被捕了。”我说：“我不觉得。”他说：“是的，我确定。”然后他把我铐在车上，把我关进警车后座。

00:36:43.300 --> 00:37:10.280
<v Mike Birbiglia>Here's what I didn't know about when you get arrested. This is a revelation. They don't have to take you to jail right away. So I'm in cuffs, watching through the windshield, as this guy is just hanging out with his cop buddies, having a great time, laughing, like, "Hahaha! The Mets!" or whatever. "Ah, I arrested this guy for nuttin'!" You know?
<v Mike Birbiglia>这是我被捕时不知道的一件事。这是一个启发。他们不必立刻把你带去监狱。所以我戴着手铐，透过挡风玻璃看着，那个家伙只是和他的警察哥们闲逛，玩得很开心，大笑，就像，“哈哈哈！大都会队！”什么的。“啊，我没理由地逮捕了这个家伙！”你知道吗？

00:37:10.280 --> 00:37:44.370
<v Mike Birbiglia>And so I'm afraid. And then I'm sweaty. And then I'm itchy. I had an itch on my face. And I didn't have any extremities available to scratch the itch. And so I'm looking around, like the character from No Country for Old Men, and I see a door handle. And I'm like, OK, I can work with this. And I reach my face over. And I'm scratching it against the door handle.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我感到害怕。然后我开始流汗。然后我开始发痒。我的脸上有个痒点，但我没有多余的肢体可以去挠那个痒。所以我四处张望，就像《老无所依》里的角色，我看到了一个门把手。我想，好的，这招能行。于是我把脸凑过去，对着门把手挠。

00:37:44.370 --> 00:38:06.490
<v Mike Birbiglia>But that just creates more itches-- on my face, on my shoulder, on my chin. And then I'm sort of writhing around in circles in the back of the cruiser like a dog trying to shake his medical cone. And the cop looks back. He's like, "We got a live one!"
<v Mike Birbiglia>但这只会造成更多的痒——在脸上，肩膀上，下巴上。然后我在警车后座像一只试图摆脱脖子上伊丽莎白圈的狗一样扭来扭去。警察往后看。他说：“我们抓到一个活宝！”

00:38:06.490 --> 00:38:29.560
<v Mike Birbiglia>"What the hell are you doing?" I'm like-- (DESPERATELY)  "Will you scratch my face?" Apparently they never do that. It turns out no one's even asked before, which is crazy to me.
<v Mike Birbiglia>“你到底在干什么？”我说——（绝望地）“你能帮我挠挠脸吗？”显然他们从不那样做。结果发现之前甚至没人问过，这让我觉得很疯狂。

00:38:29.560 --> 00:38:58.930
<v Mike Birbiglia>The officer booked me at the Weehawken, New Jersey jail. And he even took the shoelaces out of my shoes, because apparently it's a very common thing when people's licenses are suspended because they hang themselves. And I don't like to joke about it. It's called suspension post-suspension. All too common. Under-reported.
<v Mike Birbiglia>警官在新泽西州威霍肯（Weehawken）监狱给我办了入册手续。他甚至取下了我鞋子上的鞋带，因为显然这是当人们驾照被吊销时非常常见的事情，因为他们会自杀。我不喜欢拿这个开玩笑。这叫吊销后的吊销。非常普遍。未被充分报道。

00:38:58.930 --> 00:39:22.840
<v Mike Birbiglia>And and then he took a mug shot of me. And he knew I was-- he knew I'd performed at a comedy club that night, and so he took an extra one, and he put it in his pocket. And he goes, "This is in case you ever get famous."
<v Mike Birbiglia>然后他给我拍了一张入册照片。他知道我——他知道我那天晚上在喜剧俱乐部表演，所以他多拍了一张，放进了口袋里。他说：“这是以防你将来出名。”

00:39:22.840 --> 00:39:54.440
<v Mike Birbiglia>Which I thought was nice. 'Cause at least he's rooting for me. I mean, sure, he's arresting me now, but long-term, this guy sees it. Which is more than I can say for my mom. Thank you.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我觉得这很好。因为至少他在为我加油。我的意思是，当然，他现在逮捕了我，但长远来看，这家伙看好我。这比我妈给我的支持多多了。谢谢。

